  "text": "The work focuses on adding a position‑operator to the quantum description of a charged particle. Unlike earlier attempts, the form of this operator is obtained by comparing it with the known quantum formula for the particle’s angular momentum. From this, operators for velocity and momentum are derived, and their connection mirrors the classical relationship when no external forces act. By imposing reciprocal relations between the new position operator and the momentum operator, a condition emerges that becomes important when the particle’s finite size cannot be ignored; this resembles a condition used in theories of non‑local fields. The discussion also clarifies the role of an extra fifth coordinate that appears in certain unified field approaches. It is shown that the usual spatial coordinates remain meaningful but are insufficient for a full mechanical description; instead, mechanical quantities arise from operations on the new operators. The momentum retains its standard meaning while also forming part of a broader operator, analogous to how spin extends orbital angular momentum. The study is limited to this particle type because the derivation relies on the angular‑momentum analogy, and it does not aim to replace ordinary geometry with operator calculus. When extending to a force operator, an extra term appears that involves the time derivative of acceleration, suggesting a non‑classical contribution beyond the usual electromagnetic reaction."
